<Weblab2>
  <config><![CDATA[{
  "game_id": 74,
  "created_at": "2026-05-28T17:01:45.000Z",
  "level_num": "custom",
  "user_id": 574,
  "properties": {
    "require_edit_to_continue": true,
    "start_sources": {
      "folders": {
      },
      "files": {
        "73119945-7d22-4e24-bade-11c2c4edcfb3": {
          "id": "73119945-7d22-4e24-bade-11c2c4edcfb3",
          "name": "index.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <title>3D Interaction</title>\n    <style>\n        body { margin: 0; overflow: hidden; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; min-height: 100vh; background-color: #333; font-family: sans-serif; color: #eee; }\n        canvas { display: block; border: 2px solid #555; background-color: #000; }\n        h1 { position: absolute; top: 20px; color: #fff; text-align: center; width: 100%; }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n    <h1>3D Interaction</h1>\n    <canvas id=\"threeJsCanvas\"></canvas>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": true
        },
        "deb5ebcf-12f5-48f6-b037-4d7db2632df6": {
          "id": "deb5ebcf-12f5-48f6-b037-4d7db2632df6",
          "name": "script.js",
          "contents": "// This script simulates a basic 3D scene setup for demonstrating lighting, materials, and interaction.\n// It provides a simplified API resembling Three.js for educational purposes.\n// The actual 3D rendering is handled by the Web Lab environment.\n\n// --- Scene Setup (Simulated) ---\nconst scene = {\n    children: [], // Where objects and lights are added\n    add: function(object) {\n        this.children.push(object);\n        console.log(`Added object to scene:`, object);\n    }\n};\n\nconst camera = {\n    position: { x: 0, y: 1, z: 5 }, // Slightly above the scene\n    lookAt: { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 }\n};\n\nconst renderer = {\n    domElement: null, // Will be set after document is ready\n    setSize: function(width, height) {\n        if (this.domElement) {\n            this.domElement.width = width;\n            this.domElement.height = height;\n        }\n        console.log(`Renderer size set to ${width}x${height}`);\n    },\n    render: function(scene, camera) {\n        // This is a *highly simplified* 2D representation for visualization.\n        // In a real 3D engine, much more complex rendering would occur based on lights.\n        // console.log(\"--- \" + (new Date()).toLocaleTimeString() + \" Rendering Scene ---\"); // Commented for less console spam\n        const ctx = this.domElement.getContext('2d');\n        const width = this.domElement.width;\n        const height = this.domElement.height;\n\n        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height);\n        ctx.fillStyle = '#333'; // Dark background\n        ctx.fillRect(0, 0, width, height);\n\n        const scale = 50; // Pixels per unit\n        const offsetX = width / 2;\n        const offsetY = height / 2;\n\n        // Draw objects\n        scene.children.filter(obj => obj.type === 'Mesh').forEach(obj => {\n            ctx.save();\n            // Translate to object's 2D projected position\n            ctx.translate(offsetX + obj.position.x * scale, offsetY - obj.position.y * scale); // Y-axis inverted for canvas vs 3D\n\n            // Apply rotation for cubes (simplified for 2D visualization)\n            if (obj.geometry.type === 'BoxGeometry') {\n                ctx.rotate(obj.rotation.y); // Rotate around its center for Y-axis rotation\n            }\n            \n            ctx.fillStyle = obj.material.color;\n            ctx.strokeStyle = '#fff';\n            ctx.lineWidth = 1;\n\n            if (obj.geometry.type === 'BoxGeometry') {\n                ctx.fillRect(-25, -25, 50, 50); // Centered box\n                ctx.strokeRect(-25, -25, 50, 50);\n\n                // Simulate highlight based on roughness\n                if (obj.material.roughness < 0.3) { // Very shiny\n                    ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff'; // Bright highlight\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(15, -20, 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                } else if (obj.material.roughness < 0.7) { // Moderately shiny\n                    ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)'; // Fainter highlight\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(10, -15, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                }\n            } else if (obj.geometry.type === 'SphereGeometry') {\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 30, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n\n                // Simulate highlight based on roughness\n                if (obj.material.roughness < 0.3) { // Very shiny\n                    ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff'; // Bright highlight\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(15, -15, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                } else if (obj.material.roughness < 0.7) { // Moderately shiny\n                    ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)'; // Fainter highlight\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(10, -10, 12, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                }\n            } else if (obj.geometry.type === 'ConeGeometry') {\n                const coneHeight = obj.geometry.height * scale;\n                const coneRadius = obj.geometry.radius * scale;\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.moveTo(0, -coneHeight / 2); // Top point\n                ctx.lineTo(-coneRadius, coneHeight / 2); // Bottom left\n                ctx.lineTo(coneRadius, coneHeight / 2); // Bottom right\n                ctx.closePath();\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n\n                if (obj.material.roughness < 0.3) {\n                    ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff';\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(0, -10, 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                } else if (obj.material.roughness < 0.7) {\n                    ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)';\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(0, -5, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                }\n            } else if (obj.geometry.type === 'CylinderGeometry') {\n                const cylHeight = obj.geometry.height * scale;\n                const cylRadius = obj.geometry.radiusTop * scale; // Assuming radiusTop == radiusBottom\n                const ellipseYOffset = cylHeight / 2;\n                const ellipseHeightFactor = 0.3; // Make it look elliptical\n\n                // Draw the body (rectangle)\n                ctx.fillRect(-cylRadius, -ellipseYOffset, cylRadius * 2, cylHeight);\n                ctx.strokeRect(-cylRadius, -ellipseYOffset, cylRadius * 2, cylHeight);\n\n                // Draw the top ellipse\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.ellipse(0, -ellipseYOffset, cylRadius, cylRadius * ellipseHeightFactor, 0, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n\n                // Draw the bottom ellipse\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.ellipse(0, ellipseYOffset, cylRadius, cylRadius * ellipseHeightFactor, 0, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n\n                if (obj.material.roughness < 0.3) {\n                    ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff';\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(cylRadius * 0.7, -ellipseYOffset - (cylRadius * ellipseHeightFactor / 2), 6, 0, Math.PI * 2); // Highlight on top face\n                    ctx.fill();\n                } else if (obj.material.roughness < 0.7) {\n                    ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)';\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(cylRadius * 0.5, -ellipseYOffset - (cylRadius * ellipseHeightFactor / 3), 10, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                }\n            }\n            ctx.restore();\n        });\n\n        // Draw lights (simplified indicators)\n        scene.children.filter(obj => obj.type && obj.type.includes('Light')).forEach(obj => {\n            ctx.save();\n            ctx.fillStyle = obj.color;\n            ctx.strokeStyle = '#fff';\n            ctx.lineWidth = 1;\n\n            if (obj.type === 'PointLight' && obj.position) {\n                ctx.translate(offsetX + obj.position.x * scale, offsetY - obj.position.y * scale);\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n                ctx.fillStyle = '#000'; // Text color\n                ctx.fillText('P', -3, 3); // Label for Point Light\n            } else if (obj.type === 'DirectionalLight' && obj.direction) {\n                // Indicate a directional light from a corner, with an arrow\n                ctx.translate(width * 0.15, height * 0.15);\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n                ctx.fillStyle = '#000'; // Text color\n                ctx.fillText('D', -3, 3); // Label for Directional Light\n                // Draw an arrow indicating direction\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.moveTo(0, 0);\n                // Simplify direction representation for 2D. Let's just point from origin of marker\n                const arrowLength = 20;\n                ctx.lineTo(obj.direction.x * arrowLength, -obj.direction.y * arrowLength); // Y inverted\n                ctx.strokeStyle = obj.color;\n                ctx.stroke();\n            } else if (obj.type === 'AmbientLight') {\n                // Indicate ambient light with a larger circle or fill\n                ctx.translate(width * 0.85, height * 0.15);\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n                ctx.fillStyle = '#000'; // Text color\n                ctx.fillText('A', -3, 3); // Label for Ambient Light\n            }\n            ctx.restore();\n        });\n    }\n};\n\n// --- Helper for Vector-like operations (simulated) ---\nfunction Vector3(x = 0, y = 0, z = 0) {\n    this.x = x;\n    this.y = y;\n    this.z = z;\n}\nVector3.prototype.set = function(x, y, z) {\n    this.x = x;\n    this.y = y;\n    this.z = z;\n    return this;\n};\nVector3.prototype.normalize = function() {\n    const length = Math.sqrt(this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y + this.z * this.z);\n    if (length > 0) {\n        this.x /= length;\n        this.y /= length;\n        this.z /= length;\n    }\n    return this;\n};\n\n\n// --- Geometry and Material (Simulated) ---\nfunction BoxGeometry(width, height, depth) {\n    this.type = 'BoxGeometry';\n    this.width = width; this.height = height; this.depth = depth;\n}\n\nfunction SphereGeometry(radius, widthSegments, heightSegments) {\n    this.type = 'SphereGeometry';\n    this.radius = radius;\n}\n\nfunction ConeGeometry(radius, height, radialSegments) {\n    this.type = 'ConeGeometry';\n    this.radius = radius;\n    this.height = height;\n}\n\nfunction CylinderGeometry(radiusTop, radiusBottom, height, radialSegments) {\n    this.type = 'CylinderGeometry';\n    this.radiusTop = radiusTop;\n    this.radiusBottom = radiusBottom;\n    this.height = height;\n}\n\nfunction MeshStandardMaterial(parameters) {\n    this.type = 'MeshStandardMaterial';\n    // Convert Three.js-like hex color to CSS color string\n    this.color = parameters.color ? `#${(parameters.color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}` : '#ffffff';\n    this.roughness = parameters.roughness !== undefined ? parameters.roughness : 0.5; // Default mid-roughness\n    this.metalness = parameters.metalness !== undefined ? parameters.metalness : 0.0; // Default non-metal\n}\n\nfunction Mesh(geometry, material) {\n    this.type = 'Mesh';\n    this.geometry = geometry;\n    this.material = material;\n    this.position = new Vector3();\n    this.rotation = new Vector3(); // For completeness, though only Y is visually simulated in 2D for BoxGeometry\n}\n\n// --- Light Types (Simulated) ---\nfunction AmbientLight(color = 0x404040, intensity = 1) { // Default to a dim grey\n    this.type = 'AmbientLight';\n    this.color = `#${(color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}`; // Convert hex to css color\n    this.intensity = intensity;\n}\n\nfunction PointLight(color = 0xffffff, intensity = 1, distance = 0, decay = 1) {\n    this.type = 'PointLight';\n    this.color = `#${(color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}`;\n    this.intensity = intensity;\n    this.position = new Vector3();\n}\n\nfunction DirectionalLight(color = 0xffffff, intensity = 1) {\n    this.type = 'DirectionalLight';\n    this.color = `#${(color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}`;\n    this.intensity = intensity;\n    // In Three.js, direction is implied by position vs. target.\n    // Here, we'll give it an explicit 'direction' vector for simplicity.\n    this.direction = new Vector3(0, -1, 0); // Default to shining down\n}\n\n// --- Global state for interaction ---\nconst keysPressed = {}; // Stores which keys are currently held down\nlet mouseX = 0; // Current mouse X position relative to canvas\nlet mouseY = 0; // Current mouse Y position relative to canvas\n\n// --- Initialize Scene ---\n// This runs once when the page loads\nfunction init() {\n    renderer.domElement = document.getElementById('threeJsCanvas');\n    if (!renderer.domElement) {\n        console.error(\"Canvas element not found!\");\n        return;\n    }\n    renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth * 0.8, window.innerHeight * 0.8); // Adjust size for better fit\n\n    // --- Add event listeners for interaction ---\n    window.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {\n        keysPressed[event.key] = true;\n        // console.log(`Key down: ${event.key}`); // Uncomment for debugging key presses\n    });\n    window.addEventListener('keyup', (event) => {\n        keysPressed[event.key] = false;\n        // console.log(`Key up: ${event.key}`); // Uncomment for debugging key releases\n    });\n    window.addEventListener('mousemove', (event) => {\n        // Get mouse position relative to the canvas\n        const canvasRect = renderer.domElement.getBoundingClientRect();\n        mouseX = event.clientX - canvasRect.left;\n        mouseY = event.clientY - canvasRect.top;\n        // console.log(`Mouse: (${mouseX}, ${mouseY})`); // Uncomment for debugging mouse position\n    });\n\n    // Create objects with initial materials\n    // The 'cube' will be controlled by keyboard\n    const cube = new Mesh(\n        new BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1),\n        new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xff0000, roughness: 0.1, metalness: 0.9 }) // Shiny metallic red\n    );\n    cube.position.set(-1.5, 0, 0); // Position the cube to the left\n    scene.add(cube);\n\n    // The 'sphere' will be controlled by mouse\n    const sphere = new Mesh(\n        new SphereGeometry(0.7, 32, 32),\n        new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x0000ff, roughness: 0.8, metalness: 0.0 }) // Dull plastic blue\n    );\n    sphere.position.set(1.5, 0, 0); // Position the sphere to the right\n    scene.add(sphere);\n\n    const cone = new Mesh(\n        new ConeGeometry(0.7, 1.5, 32),\n        new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x00ff00, roughness: 0.4, metalness: 0.2 }) // Moderately shiny green\n    );\n    cone.position.set(0, 0.75, -2); // Position further back\n    scene.add(cone);\n\n    const cylinder = new Mesh(\n        new CylinderGeometry(0.5, 0.5, 1, 32),\n        new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xffff00, roughness: 0.9, metalness: 0.0 }) // Very rough yellow\n    );\n    cylinder.position.set(-2, 0.5, 1); // Position to the left-front\n    scene.add(cylinder);\n\n    // --- Lights from the previous level (already configured) ---\n    const ambientLight = new AmbientLight(0x404040, 0.5);\n    scene.add(ambientLight);\n\n    const pointLight = new PointLight(0xffffff, 1);\n    pointLight.position.set(0, 2, 2);\n    scene.add(pointLight);\n\n    const directionalLight = new DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 0.8);\n    directionalLight.direction.set(1, 1, 1).normalize();\n    scene.add(directionalLight);\n    // --- End of Lights ---\n\n    // Start the animation loop after initial setup\n    animate();\n}\n\n// This function runs repeatedly to update and render the scene (about 60 times per second)\nfunction animate() {\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate); // Request the next frame to continue the animation loop\n\n    // --- Student Interaction Code Goes Here ---\n    // Task 1: Keyboard control for the cube\n    // Use arrow keys to rotate the cube.\n    // 'ArrowLeft' and 'ArrowRight' can rotate around the Y-axis (horizontal spin).\n    // 'ArrowUp' and 'ArrowDown' can rotate around the X-axis (forward/backward tilt).\n    if (keysPressed['ArrowLeft']) {\n        cube.rotation.y -= 0.05; \n    }\n    if (keysPressed['ArrowRight']) {\n        cube.rotation.y += 0.05;\n    }\n    if (keysPressed['ArrowUp']) {\n        cube.rotation.x -= 0.05; // Rotate around X axis (pitch)\n    }\n    if (keysPressed['ArrowDown']) {\n        cube.rotation.x += 0.05; // Rotate around X axis (pitch)\n    }\n\n    // Task 2: Mouse control for the sphere\n    // Move the sphere's X and Y position based on the mouse cursor.\n    // You'll need to convert mouse coordinates (pixels from top-left) to scene coordinates (units).\n    const canvas = renderer.domElement;\n    const width = canvas.width;\n    const height = canvas.height;\n    const scale = 50; // Pixels per scene unit (defined in renderer)\n    const offsetX = width / 2; // Canvas center X in pixels\n    const offsetY = height / 2; // Canvas center Y in pixels\n\n    // Calculate target X and Y positions for the sphere in scene units.\n    // mouseX and mouseY are relative to the canvas's top-left corner.\n    // We want them relative to the center, and the Y-axis inverted to match 3D conventions.\n    sphere.position.x = (mouseX - offsetX) / scale;\n    sphere.position.y = (offsetY - mouseY) / scale; // Invert Y: higher mouseY (lower screen) means lower 3D y-position\n\n    // --- End Student Interaction Code ---\n\n    // Render the scene with updated object positions/rotations for this frame\n    renderer.render(scene, camera);\n}\n\n// Initialize the scene when the script loads\ninit();",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": false
        }
      },
      "openFiles": [
        "73119945-7d22-4e24-bade-11c2c4edcfb3",
        "deb5ebcf-12f5-48f6-b037-4d7db2632df6"
      ]
    },
    "long_instructions": "You've built up your 3D scene with lights and materials. Now, let's bring it to life with user interaction! In this level, you'll modify `script.js` to allow users to control objects using their keyboard and mouse.\r\n\r\n1.  **Open `script.js`:** Locate the sections marked `// Global state for interaction`, `// Add event listeners for interaction`, and `// --- Student Interaction Code Goes Here ---`.\r\n2.  **Explore the Code:** Notice the `keysPressed` object and `mouseX`, `mouseY` variables that track user input. Also, see the `window.addEventListener` calls that update these variables when keys are pressed or the mouse moves. The `animate()` function now continuously updates and renders the scene.\r\n3.  **Control the Cube with Keyboard:** Inside the `animate()` function, add `if` statements that check the `keysPressed` object. When an arrow key is pressed, modify the `cube.rotation.x` and `cube.rotation.y` properties. For example, `cube.rotation.y -= 0.05;` will rotate the cube around its vertical axis. Experiment with different keys and rotation amounts.\r\n4.  **Move the Sphere with Mouse:** Also within `animate()`, calculate the `sphere.position.x` and `sphere.position.y` based on the `mouseX` and `mouseY` variables. You'll need to convert the mouse's pixel coordinates into the scene's 3D units. The comments provide a good starting point for mapping these values.\r\n5.  **Experiment:** Try making other objects interactive! Can you make the cone spin automatically, or the cylinder jump when a specific key is pressed? Have fun exploring how to add dynamic behavior to your 3D world!",
    "generate_outline": "Students will implement simple user interactions in their Web Lab 2 3D scene. They should be able to rotate an object using keyboard arrow keys or move an object based on mouse input."
  },
